ecific degree to work in sales, retail, or commercial services? No, this industry is open to graduates from all disciplines . However, degrees in the following areas may be advantageous:   Business & Management Marketing & Digital Retail Economics or Finance Fashion, Merchandising & Design (for fashion retail roles) Data Analytics or IT (for e-commerce and commercial roles)   Retail management and commercial graduate schemes focus more on leadership potential, people skills, and business acumen rather than specific degrees. What are the key skills needed for success in this sector? Communication & Negotiation – Sales and commercial roles require strong persuasion skills. Customer Service & Relationship Building – Key for both B2B and retail careers. Analytical & Data Skills – Buying, merchandising, and digital sales require a data-driven mindset. Resilience & Target-Driven Attitude – Sales roles can be fast-paced and competitive. Commercial Awareness – Understanding market trends and business strategy is crucial. Leadership & Team Management – Important for retail management roles. How much do graduate jobs pay in this sector? Salaries vary depending on the role and company:   Sales Executive / Business Development: £25,000 – £35,000 (plus commission) Retail Graduate Schemes: £28,000 – £40,000 Buying & Merchandising Roles: £25,000 – £35,000 E-commerce & Digital Retail: £26,000 – £38,000 Luxury & Fashion Retail Management: £24,000 – £32,000   Sales roles often have commission structures, meaning earnings can increase significantly based on performance. How competitive is this industry? Retail graduate schemes (especially in major supermarkets and luxury brands) can be highly competitive due to strong demand. B2B and SaaS (Software-as-a-Service) sales roles have a high demand for graduates . Buying & merchandising roles are more competitive, as many candidates are drawn to these positions. Retail management and store leadership roles tend to be less competitive but re