education graduate jobs & schemes 2027 Search Teaching & education graduate jobs & schemes 2027 Create tracker New in Jobs & opportunities Filters 2 People also searched for: Teaching & education graduate jobs & schemes 2027 Graduate jobs & schemes 2027 Graduate jobs & schemes in Aberdeen 2027 Accounting graduate jobs & schemes 2027 Banking graduate jobs & schemes 2027 Graduate jobs & schemes in Belfast 2027 FAQs: What types of graduate jobs are available in teaching and education? Careers in education go beyond traditional teaching roles. Here are some key areas:   Teaching Roles Primary School Teacher Secondary School Teacher (subject-specific) Special Educational Needs (SEN) Teacher Early Years Educator   Education Support & Leadership Teaching Assistant (TA) / Learning Support Assistant (LSA) Pastoral Support Officer School Administrator Education Consultant   Alternative Education Careers Private Tutor Educational Psychology Assistant EdTech Specialist (e.g., online learning platforms) Training & Development (corporate education)   Many graduates enter teacher training schemes or work in education-related sectors like policy, administration, and technology . What are the main graduate routes into teaching? There are multiple paths to becoming a teacher in the UK:   ✔ Postgraduate Certificate in Education (PGCE) – A one-year course with school placements, required for teaching in most schools. ✔ School-Centred Initial Teacher Training (SCITT) – A school-based alternative to a PGCE. ✔ Teach First – A two-year leadership-focused graduate scheme, placing trainees in schools while they train. ✔ Postgraduate Teaching Apprenticeship – Work-based teacher training with a salary. ✔ Qualified Teacher Status (QTS) Only Programmes – Some routes offer QTS without a PGCE. ✔ Researchers in Schools (RIS) – A programme for PhD graduates to enter teaching.   For nursery and early years roles , a degree in Early Childhood Studies or a Level 3 qualification (e.g., Early Years Educator) is often needed. Do I need a specific degree to become a teacher? It depends on the subject and age group:   Primary teaching – Any degree is accepted, but subjects like Education, English, or STEM are beneficial. Secondary teaching – A degree in the subject you want to teach is typically required (e.g., Maths for Maths teaching). Specialist roles (e.g., SEN, Early Years) may require specific qualifications.   If your degree is unrelated, you can take a Subject Knowledge Enhancement (SKE) course before teacher training. What are the key skills needed for success in teaching and education? Communication & Presentation Skills – Essential for engaging students and explaining complex topics. Patience & Empathy – Understanding students' needs and supporting their development. Classroom Management & Leadership – Keeping lessons structured and handling behaviour. Adaptability & Creativity – Responding to challenges and making lessons engaging. Organisation & Time Management – Handling lesson planning, marking, and assessments. How much do graduate jobs in teaching pay? Salaries depend on location, experience, and school type.   State Schools (England & Wales, 2024 figures) Trainee Teacher (Teach First, SCITT, PGCE with bursary): £26,000 – £30,000 Newly Qualified Teacher (NQT / ECT Year 1): £30,000+ (£36,745+ in London) Experienced Teacher: £36,000 – £46,000 Head of Department / Senior Teacher: £45,000 – £65,000 Headteacher / Principal: £70,000 – £120,000+   Independent & International Schools Salaries vary, with some offering higher pay but different benefits (e.g., housing in international schools).   Private Tutoring & Alternative Routes Tutors can earn £20–£50 per hour , depending on experience and demand. Education consultants and trainers may earn £30,000 – £60,000+ . How competitive is teaching as a career? High demand for teachers in STEM subjects, computing, languages, and special education. Teach First and competitive PGCEs (e.g., Oxford, UCL) have rigorous entry processes. Primary school teaching can be more competitive in some regions. Alternative education roles (e.g., educational psychology) often require postgraduate study. How can I improve my chances of getting a job in teaching or education? ✔ Gain classroom experience – Volunteering, tutoring, or working as a Teaching Assistant helps. ✔ Develop strong subject knowledge – Especially for secondary education. ✔ Enhance your leadership & teamwork skills – Teaching involves managing groups effectively. ✔ Understand safeguarding & child protection – Many roles require DBS checks and safeguarding training. ✔ Stay informed about education trends – Read about new teaching methods, policies, and curriculum updates. targetjobs Legal Legal Privacy policy Terms and conditions for employers Terms and conditions for students About our cookies Manage cookie settings Accessibility GTI Sites GTI Sites targetjobsawards.co.uk undergraduateoftheyear.com targetapprenticeships.co.uk gradireland.com cibyl.com groupgti.com Useful Links Useful Links About us Sitemap Graduate jobs & schemes Organisation A-Z Career advisers - how to get involved Are you hiring? Careers guides and publications CV and covering letter templates Follow Us Follow Us Facebook LinkedIn Twitter YouTube Instagram TikTok © Copyright 2026 GTI Futures Ltd. Registered in England No. 2347472. First Floor, 240 Blackfriars Road, London, SE1 8NW, UK. v1.6.101",
